                <pb facs="0241" n="207" xml:id="pg207"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">No. 161.</note>
                <head>Mr. Bancroft to Mr.
                        Fish.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 135.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation,</hi><hi
                                rendition="#italic"> Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1870-09-16"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >September</hi> 16, 1870</date>. (Received October
                    3.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Having received a telegram from Commander
                    Luce, of the Juniata, that the French blockade of the Elbe and Weser had been
                    raised on the 11th, I telegraphed the news to you yesterday.</p>
                <p>To-day I have to inform you that the French steamer Pereire, which on its voyage
                    from New York touched at Brest, had on board for Havre a considerable number of
                    Remington guns. This fact is also known to the government here. * * * * *</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>GEO. BANCROFT.</signed></closer>
            </div>
